{#
    The entries of a table of contents that stands *in* a document.

    Separate from `menu-item.html.twig` on purpose, and this is the whole of
    the difference: that one writes the rail, which is where a reader goes
    next, and this one writes a list of places on the page they are already
    on. The core sends both through `menu-level.html.twig`, so a theme that
    overrides that one file has said the same thing about both — which is how
    `.. contents::` came out as a row of filled rail items, every one of them
    marked as the current page.

    The link is built here rather than taken from the renderer. `renderLink`
    answers `#` for the document being rendered — its way of saying "you are
    here" — and a section of that document is that answer plus the anchor. Take
    the answer alone and every entry of a local contents points at nothing,
    which is exactly what they did.

    Recursion is the macro calling itself: a contents nests as deep as the
    document does, and the depth is the author's to choose with `:depth:`.
#}
{% macro entries(items, kids) %}
    {%- import _self as toc -%}
    <ul>
        {%- for item in items %}
            {%- set doc = renderLink(item.url) -%}
            {%- set href = (doc == '#' ? '' : doc) ~ (item.anchor ? '#' ~ item.anchor) -%}
            <li>
                <a href="{{ href ?: '#' }}">{{ item.value.toString }}</a>
                {%- set children = attribute(item, kids) -%}
                {%- if children %}{{ toc.entries(children, kids) }}{% endif -%}
            </li>
        {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
{% endmacro %}
